+# ADR-0017: Identity reads advisor master data from the ERP through integration
+
+- **Status:** Proposed
+- **Date:** 2026-11-04
+- **Deciders:** <names>
+- **Sources:** T3-D-005, T2-D-009, T3-D-009, ADR-0004, TD-15, [AN-902](../specs/AN-902/spec.md)
+
+## Context
+
+Head office needs the master data of an advisor as the ERP holds it: advisor number (`B` + 5 digits), given name, family name, e-mail and active flag (AN-902). The ERP port has only `readCustomer` today. Every module reaches the ERP only through the `integration` module and its port (T3-D-005), and every read records its source version and read time (T2-D-009). An advisor is a role of the person record, which `identity` owns (T3-D-009), so `identity` is the module that offers the advisor to its callers. Until now no module of `packages/core` depends on another module.
+
+## Options
+
+1. **New port operation `readAdvisor`; `identity` calls `integration.readAdvisor`.** Good: follows the ERP conventions; identity stays the owner of person and role data; one small dependency (`Pick<Integration, "readAdvisor">`). Bad: first direct dependency between two core modules.
+2. **Callers use `integration.readAdvisor` directly; no identity API.** Good: no new module dependency. Bad: the advisor is spread across two modules, and the story asks for `identity.getAdvisor`.
+3. **Extend `CustomerMasterData` with advisor fields.** Good: no new operation. Bad: mixes two ERP records; an advisor without a customer record cannot be read.
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 1. It keeps the ERP behind `integration` (T3-D-005), keeps advisor data with the module that owns roles (T3-D-009), and the dependency points from a domain module to `integration`, the direction that every later ERP read will take. The new contract is `AdvisorMasterData` (`advisorNumber`, `givenName`, `familyName`, `email`, `active`, `sourceVersion`) and `ErpPort.readAdvisor(advisorNumber)`, which returns `null` for an unknown advisor.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Other modules that need ERP data follow the same pattern: receive `Integration` (or a `Pick` of it) as a dependency, wired in `apps/worker`.
+- `integration` must not depend on `identity`, or the dependency becomes a cycle. `pnpm arch` enforces imports through `index.ts`.
+- Every ERP adapter (`erp-fake`, later `erp-nav`, `erp-bc`) must implement `readAdvisor`.
+- No copy is stored: each call reads the ERP. A read copy is a later decision.
